there were people from all walks of  French life teachers students and  lawyers joint transport and health  workers and central Paris part of a  nationwide strike protesting against the  government's plans to reform the pension  system they fear the changes would force  them to work for more years and reduce  their payouts we are here to try and  preserve our pension system which is one  of the best in Europe and we don't want  to go backwards  macrons reforms are destroying French  society it looks like we'll have to  retire even later that will have even  less money in our pension the  demonstration was mainly peaceful but  there were some clashes between police  and so-called black bloc anarchist  protesters more than 6000 police  officers were deployed in the city this  police union spokesperson said many of  them would have wanted to join the  strike yes it's difficult for us the  strike caused major disruption across  the city there was little public  transport and most schools were shut  even the eiffel tower was forced to  close the French president says the  pension system needs to be streamlined  to make it fairer and save public money  the strikes are a major test for  Emmanuel macron because he made  reforming the pension system one of his  campaign promises so if he gives in to  the protesters and scraps his reform it  will be a political failure but so far  the French president is showing no sign  of backing down this analyst says macro  has a track record of controversial  reforms since the beginning of its  mandate the government has passed  several reforms that many would have  believed would be almost impossible but  they managed to push them through  despite the challenges and risk macro  may not want to make concessions but if  the disruption continues  may have to rethink although most people  are expected to return to work this week  some transport workers say they won't  give up their fight and will continue to  strike  Natasha Butler al Jazeera Paris
 

